From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] server: Keep system APCs for a title's second process on
 PS5.

The threads of a second process of a title (patch 1011) can't be sent
signals, so send_thread_signal() fails for every one of them. queue_apc()
then failed for a system APC to such a thread whenever the thread was not
waiting on the server. Wine expects that only for a terminated process:
the APC is destroyed at once, which completes its async from inside
async_terminate(). A socket async that went this way had no reference
left when its socket was closed, and free_async_queue() released it again:
release_object() asserted (object.c:566) and the title aborted. A Steam
client in the second process hit this within a minute or two.

Such a thread keeps its unix tid while it lives and takes its system APCs
at its next wait on the server, so the APC now stays queued as long as the
thread has one. A process-wide APC that finds no thread to signal goes to
the first live thread for the same reason.

Tested on a console, Team Fortress 2 in the title's process and a Steam
client in its second process: without this patch the title aborted with
the assert above within 90 seconds; with it, three starts ran 3 to 6
minutes until they were closed. A build that logged the new paths showed
the kept system APC once, for a thread of the second process; the
process-wide fallback did not run.
---
 server/thread.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/server/thread.c b/server/thread.c
index d1c1b52..60c172d 100644
--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -1611,6 +1611,19 @@ static int queue_apc( struct process *process, struct thread *thread, struct thr
                 }
             }
         }
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+        /* the threads of a second process of the title can't be signalled
+         * (patch 1011): a live one takes the call at its next wait on the server */
+        if (!thread)
+        {
+            LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY( candidate, &process->thread_list, struct thread, proc_entry )
+            {
+                if (candidate->state == TERMINATED || candidate->unix_tid == -1) continue;
+                thread = candidate;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+#endif
         if (!thread) return 0;  /* nothing found */
         if (!(queue = get_apc_queue( thread, apc->call.type ))) return 1;
     }
@@ -1621,7 +1634,15 @@ static int queue_apc( struct process *process, struct thread *thread, struct thr
         /* send signal for system APCs if needed */
         if (queue == &thread->system_apc && list_empty( queue ) && !is_in_apc_wait( thread ))
         {
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+            /* a thread of a second process of the title can't be signalled
+             * (patch 1011) but keeps its unix tid while it lives: it takes the
+             * call at its next wait on the server. Failing here would complete an
+             * async at once, which free_async_queue() then releases again. */
+            if (!send_thread_signal( thread, SIGUSR1 ) && thread->unix_tid == -1) return 0;
+#else
             if (!send_thread_signal( thread, SIGUSR1 )) return 0;
+#endif
         }
         /* cancel a possible previous APC with the same owner */
         if (apc->owner) thread_cancel_apc( thread, apc->owner, apc->call.type );
